          "Gradient clipping by global norm prevents unstable parameter steps by scaling down gradients whenever their collective magnitude exceeds a threshold.",
          "The algorithm computes the total L2 norm by summing the squared Frobenius norms of all gradient tensors across all model parameters.",
          "The global gradient norm is the square root of this sum: g_norm = sqrt(sum(||g_i||^2)).",
          "If g_norm exceeds the clipping threshold (typically 1.0), all gradients are scaled by factor = clip_threshold / g_norm.",
          "This uniform scaling preserves the relative direction of the gradient vector in parameter space while bounding the maximum step size.",
          "In distributed training environments, computing the global gradient norm requires an All-Reduce collective across all participating accelerators.",
          "Because only a single scalar value representing the sum of squared norms is communicated, this collective incurs virtually negligible network overhead.",
          "Once the global norm is synchronized across all ranks, each GPU locally scales its assigned gradient shards using the exact same scaling ratio.",
          "This decentralized application guarantees that parameter updates remain mathematically identical across all distributed data parallel replicas.",
          "Rigorous testing confirms that global norm clipping maintains smooth optimization trajectories even during aggressive learning rate warmup phases."
